From 0aea23f6e5fc6bea8e9896382b7f5fba98536975 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felicitas Pojtinger <felicitas@pojtinger.com>
Date: Tue, 06 Oct 2026 23:28:05 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] docs: Add secrets store, basic grants system idea and CLI, agent forwarding and proxy
---
diff --git a/ROADMAP.md b/ROADMAP.md
index 3ac9b35433557936a05a1841acb9b9218492d924..6c41049cfd6cfbbc161a00d06f071580f6667e54 100644
--- a/ROADMAP.md
+++ b/ROADMAP.md
@@ -130,3 +130,76 @@ - Mount the secrets share with `ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec`.
 - Map the guest user to the host user on the secrets share, so the guest user reads the files and no other guest user does.
 - Set one explicit uid and gid map in virtiofsd, and do not rely on two matching defaults.
 - Acceptance: a host file appears in the guest with no restart.
+## Secrets store
+
+- Create one runtime secret directory on a tmpfs, with mode 0700.
+- Keep one mapping file, and give it four columns: name, mode, source, and upstream.
+- Resolve one source scheme: `keepassxc://`.
+- Read one entry through the KeePassXC Secret Service, so the KeePassXC window must be open and unlocked.
+- Write one file per secret into the secrets share.
+- Name each file after the secret, so the guest can source it as an environment variable.
+- Name one mode per row. `env` puts the secret in a file that the guest reads. `proxy` injects the secret into an outbound request on the host.
+- Send `proxy` rows to the host over the vsock proxy.
+- The host runs Caddy as a user unit, and Caddy injects the secret into the outbound request header before the request leaves.
+- Write every value with a temp file and a rename, so no reader sees a partial value.
+- Keep the mapping file in the user configuration directory, and open it with the default editor.
+- Expose one command that lists the secret names, and never the values.
+- Keep the SSH agent and the GnuPG agent out of the secret store.
+- Acceptance: the list command prints names and never values.
+
+## Grants
+
+- Add one command that grants one secret for a set duration.
+- Write the secret file, then touch one grant file that records the expiry.
+- Schedule the expiry with `systemd-run --on-active=<duration>`.
+- Add one command that revokes a grant and removes both files.
+- Add one command that reads the expiry and prints the remaining time.
+- Send a desktop notification on grant and on revoke.
+- Decide the exact grant subject types during implementation: an API key, an SSH key, or a GnuPG key.
+- Acceptance: the secret file disappears on time with no other step.
+## CLI and runner
+
+- Add one sync command that resolves every mapping row and writes every secret file.
+- Add one guest runner that loads every file in the secrets share as an environment variable, then runs the given command.
+- In the guest, load the secrets into guest units with `LoadCredential=` when a guest unit must not see the environment.
+- Add one path unit on the guest mount, and let it restart the units that consume secrets.
+- Keep the CLI as one program with the subcommands `sync`, `allow`, `revoke`, `status`, and `ls`.
+- Put the program in the user path, so the guest runner and the units can call it.
+- Acceptance: the guest runner prints the variable in the guest.
+## Agent forwarding
+
+- Run one dedicated SSH agent on the host, and keep its socket in the runtime directory.
+- Load only the guest keys into that agent.
+- Load each key with `ssh-add -c`, so the agent asks before it uses the key.
+- Load each key with `ssh-add -t 1h`, so the agent drops it after one hour.
+- Do not set `-h` for now. Accept that the forwarded key can sign for any host that already trusts its public key.
+- Rely on the public key itself for the boundary. Add it only to the systems that should accept it.
+- Run one plain `ssh-agent` as the dedicated agent, and point `SSH_ASKPASS` at a GTK askpass, so the confirm prompt appears on the host.
+- Forward the agent socket to the guest over vsock with one socat pair.
+- Forward the GnuPG extra socket to the guest over vsock with one socat pair.
+- Run `pinentry-gnome3` on the host for the GnuPG prompt, so the prompt appears there and not in the guest.
+- Keep no private key in the guest.
+- Acceptance: a guest push prompts on the host, and the guest holds no private key.
+## Proxy
+
+- Run Caddy on the host as a user unit.
+- Listen on one port on the host, and reach it from the guest as `http://host.internal:4000`.
+- Add `admin off` to the global options in the Caddyfile, so no control endpoint listens on the host.
+- Point Caddy at one directory of drop-in files, and let the sync command rewrite the main Caddyfile.
+- Apply the change with `systemctl --user reload phial-caddy`, and set `ExecReload` in the unit to send the reload signal.
+- Make sure that Caddy is 2.11 or newer, because `SIGUSR1` reloads only from that version. Restart the unit on an older build.
+- Do not rely on `--watch`, because Caddy watches the main config file and not the files it imports.
+- Write one drop-in file per proxy row when you add a provider.
+- Remove the drop-in file when you revoke the row.
+- Match the grant file for the row before Caddy injects the secret.
+- Inject the secret into the `Authorization` header of the outbound request.
+- Treat a bearer API key as the common case, and plan for one custom provider per service when that header is not enough.
+- Expect API keys for most providers, and add a drop-in and a mapping row for each custom one.
+- Keep the secret on the host for every provider that allows it, so the guest holds only a placeholder.
+- Put the secret in the guest only when host-side injection cannot work, for example an OIDC flow or a client that signs its own requests.
+- Avoid that path. It is the exception, and it gives the guest the real value.
+- Respond with 403 when the grant file is missing.
+- Add one default handler that answers 404, so an unmatched path returns 404 on purpose.
+- Point the guest at the host path that names the row, and use a placeholder token in the guest.
+- Acceptance: revoking the grant returns 403 on the next request.
+

